            {
              "id": "eada670c-a132-39b9-966b-49069cd1eb51",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential for misuse, even if the function is used correctly. In C++, this often refers to functions that do not perform bounds checking, such as `strcpy()`, `sprintf()`, and `gets()`. \n\nIn the provided code, `fprintf(stdout, format, args)`, the vulnerability arises from the use of a format string that is not a constant, which can lead to a format string attack if an attacker can control the format string. This can lead to a variety of attacks, including reading from arbitrary memory locations or executing arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncpy()` instead of `strcpy()`, or `snprintf()` instead of `sprintf()`. \n\nIn the case of `fprintf()`, it is recommended to always use a constant format string. If user input is used in the output, it should be included as a separate argument, not part of the format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nfprintf(stdout, format, args);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nfprintf(stdout, \"%s\", format);\n```\n\nThis ensures that the format string is a constant, and any user input is treated as a string to be printed, not part of the format string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the `<stdio.h>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "71d98922-9518-34e3-91f6-4f5da5dda3c3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. In this case, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For `printf`, a safer alternative is `snprintf`, which includes a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows. Also, always control the format string and never pass user input directly to it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `printf` function with `snprintf` function:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
