            {
              "id": "8272e065-67e5-3e7e-a24c-0a7fb51d620d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. In this case, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For `printf`, a safer alternative is `snprintf`, which includes a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows. Also, always control the format string and never pass user input directly to it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `printf` function with `snprintf` function:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "25fd99d3-32b0-3ddd-88e1-6d716ebed015",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `strlen` function in C++. This function is used to calculate the length of a string, but it can lead to a buffer overflow if the string is not null-terminated. This can happen if the string is read from an untrusted source without proper validation or sanitization. The buffer overflow can lead to unexpected behavior, crashes, or even code execution, depending on the context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that your strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character (`'\\0'`) at the end, or by manually adding one before calling `strlen`.\n\nIn addition, you should always validate and sanitize your inputs, especially if they come from an untrusted source. This can help prevent buffer overflows and other types of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a possible fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\nif (value != nullptr) {\n    size_t len = 0;\n    while (value[len] != '\\0') {\n        ++len;\n    }\n    str = {value, len};\n} else {\n    str = {value, 0};\n}\n```\n\nThis code manually calculates the length of the string, stopping when it encounters a null character. This avoids the potential buffer overflow that could occur with `strlen`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `std::strlen`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible. Always check the validity of the links before using them."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3cdea013-5041-3fc2-9631-8e6f534b64ca",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen` on strings that may not be null-terminated. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that take the size of the buffer as an argument, such as `strnlen`, which will not read past the specified length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strlen`, use `strnlen`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nconst char* str_value = \"example\";\nstd::size_t max_length = 100; // Maximum expected length of the string\nstr = { str_value, std::strnlen(str_value, max_length) };\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7fc2bb6e-3307-3e57-b677-1744cb53e42b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be risky and can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `std::memmove` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if not used carefully.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using risky C standard library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library. For instance, instead of `std::memmove`, consider using `std::copy` or `std::copy_n` from `<algorithm>` which are safer as they work with iterators and can take advantage of C++ type safety.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a possible fix using `std::copy_n`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n\n// Assuming get(p) returns a pointer to Char and width and n are of appropriate types\nstd::copy_n(get(p), n, get(p) + (width - n) / 2);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `std::memmove`\n\nThe fixed code requires the following library:\n\n- `<algorithm>` for `std::copy_n`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "13b911e1-392f-35f9-b2ce-3265ec6c5e9e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The verdict is supported by the classification indicating undefined behavior risk and the explicit 'GENUINE' verdict. The analysis also detected that both the source and destination may be null pointers, and there is no evidence of protective checks to prevent this. Additionally, the dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior or security issues. The presence of an explicit null-terminator assignment after the copy does not mitigate the undefined behavior risk from overlapping memory regions.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the risk of undefined behavior caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the string copy operation, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a memory-safe function that supports overlapping regions, such as `memmove`, instead of `strncpy`. Additionally, add explicit null pointer checks for both the source and destination to prevent null pointer dereference vulnerabilities.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (event.message != nullptr && event_buffer[i].message != nullptr) {\n    // If overlap is possible, use memmove instead of strncpy\n    memmove(event.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination if required\n    event.message[AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9d61ad04-d6ae-3588-be1a-edd3a0844934",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy operation where the source and destination buffers may overlap in memory. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C standard, as the function used does not support overlapping memory regions. The analysis indicates that both the source and destination may be null, and there is no evidence of checks to prevent this. Additionally, the destination buffer's size is not explicitly known, and the count argument is not directly derived from the destination's capacity. The presence of a possible overlap is confirmed by data flow analysis, and the risk is further increased by the lack of explicit guards against null pointers. These factors make this a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the risk of undefined behavior when copying strings where the source and destination may overlap, first ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use a memory-safe function that supports overlapping regions, such as `memmove`, instead of `strncpy`. Additionally, add explicit null pointer checks for both the source and destination before performing the copy to prevent null pointer dereference.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (event_record.message != nullptr && event_buffer[i].message != nullptr) {\n    memmove(event_record.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination if copying strings\n    event_record.message[AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis approach ensures that the copy is safe even if the buffers overlap and prevents undefined behavior. Always validate buffer sizes and ensure proper null-termination for string data.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5f88a8d4-c611-36af-8fea-44fa5e412a55",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the data flow analysis. Additionally, the source may be null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. There is no explicit null-termination of the destination buffer after the copy, and no guard is present to ensure safe operation. The destination is not a pointer-based member, which reduces the risk of null destination, but the overlap and possible null source make this a high-confidence issue that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior caused by overlapping memory regions, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a memory-safe function such as `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, always ensure the destination buffer is null-terminated after the copy to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n**Remediation example:**\n\n```cpp\n// Check for overlap and use memmove if necessary\nif ((char*)event_data[num_elem].message == (char*)event_description_(args->events[e])) {\n    // If source and destination are the same, no action needed\n} else {\n    memmove(event_data[num_elem].message, event_description_(args->events[e]), MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE);\n    // Ensure null-termination\n    event_data[num_elem].message[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nIf you are certain that the source and destination never overlap, document this assumption clearly in the code and add an assertion to enforce it at runtime:\n\n```cpp\nassert(event_data[num_elem].message != event_description_(args->events[e]));\nstrncpy(event_data[num_elem].message, event_description_(args->events[e]), MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE);\nevent_data[num_elem].message[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b304e9f9-e1d0-316f-b7fd-54aa3ba0d862",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security problems. The `getenv` function is one such function. It returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to security vulnerabilities if the returned string is modified, or if the program does not handle the possibility that `getenv` could return a null pointer if the specified environment variable does not exist.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `getenv` function. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that are less prone to security issues. Always validate and sanitize input, and handle the possibility of null pointers. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, you can use a safer alternative like `getenv_s`. Here is an example of how to use it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nchar* value;\nsize_t requiredSize;\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, \"ENV_VAR\");\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    printf(\"The environment variable does not exist.\\n\");\n}\nelse\n{\n    value = (char*) malloc(requiredSize * sizeof(char));\n    if (value != NULL)\n    {\n        getenv_s(&requiredSize, value, requiredSize, \"ENV_VAR\");\n        printf(\"The value of the environment variable is: %s\\n\", value);\n    }\n    free(value);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `getenv_s` is used to safely get the value of an environment variable. It checks if the environment variable exists and handles the possibility of a null pointer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "70153a3c-d1db-3f8e-8afd-ad1e8d114aff",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee null-termination of the destination string when the source string length is greater than or equal to the size parameter. This can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, alter the intended control flow, read sensitive information, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination of the destination string. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `std::string` class or the `strlcpy` function if available. Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string and the null-terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with `strlcpy` or `std::string`. Here is an example of how to do it with `std::string`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string event_message(event_record.message);\nif (event_message.size() > AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR) {\n    event_message.resize(AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<string>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "52c834d2-8854-3355-907d-fc6d48da689f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other vulnerabilities. The `gmtime` function is one such function. It converts a time_t value to a struct tm and returns a pointer to it. The problem is that this function is not thread-safe, because it uses a static buffer to hold the result. If the function is called from multiple threads, it can cause data races and undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using unsafe C functions like `gmtime`. Instead, use safer alternatives that are thread-safe and less prone to causing vulnerabilities. In the case of `gmtime`, a safer alternative is `gmtime_r`, which is reentrant and does not use a static buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to replace `gmtime` with `gmtime_r`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <time.h>\n\nvoid print_time(time_t time) {\n    struct tm result;\n    gmtime_r(&time, &result);\n    printf(\"%s\", asctime(&result));\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `gmtime_r` is used instead of `gmtime`. The result is stored in a local struct tm, which avoids the data races that can occur with `gmtime`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- time.h\n- stdio.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189",
                  "52c834d2-8854-3355-907d-fc6d48da689f"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b917e583-43f8-3e81-9358-2a4f78a8a5cf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions within C++ code. These functions are often older and do not include protections against common security vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows or format string vulnerabilities. \n\nIn this case, the function `localtime` is considered insecure. The `localtime` function converts a time_t value to a tm structure as local time. It returns a pointer to static data and hence isn't thread-safe. If the function is called from multiple threads, the data can be overwritten leading to incorrect results.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `localtime`, a safer alternative is `localtime_r`, which is a reentrant version of `localtime`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to replace `localtime` with `localtime_r`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <time.h>\n\nvoid printLocalTime(time_t timer) {\n    struct tm result;\n    localtime_r(&timer, &result);\n    printf(\"%s\", asctime(&result));\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `localtime_r` is used instead of `localtime`. The second argument to `localtime_r` is a pointer to a `tm` structure that `localtime_r` fills in with the broken-down time.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- time.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189",
                  "b917e583-43f8-3e81-9358-2a4f78a8a5cf"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "85380527-6a29-360d-b440-1ca6faa05438",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions within C++ code. These functions are often older and do not include protections against common security vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows or format string vulnerabilities. \n\nIn this case, the function `localtime` is considered insecure. The `localtime` function converts a time_t value to a tm structure as local time. It returns a pointer to static data and hence isn't thread-safe. If the function is called from multiple threads, the data can be overwritten leading to incorrect results.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `localtime`, a safer alternative is `localtime_r`, which is a reentrant version of `localtime`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to replace `localtime` with `localtime_r`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <time.h>\n\nvoid printLocalTime(time_t timer) {\n    struct tm result;\n    localtime_r(&timer, &result);\n    printf(\"%s\", asctime(&result));\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `localtime_r` is used instead of `localtime`. The second argument to `localtime_r` is a pointer to a `tm` structure that `localtime_r` fills in with the broken-down time.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- time.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
