            {
              "id": "8638ef44-7e27-3667-bd5d-9e42b365713f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the `gmtime()` function is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to data races if used in multi-threaded applications.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in multi-threaded applications. Instead, use their thread-safe counterparts. In this case, instead of `gmtime()`, use `gmtime_r()`, which is a thread-safe version of `gmtime()`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fix for the vulnerability sink:\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n\ntime_t now_t;\nstruct tm timeinfo;\n\ngmtime_r(&now_t, &timeinfo);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- ctime\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
