            {
              "id": "5a26ae69-f4b4-3987-8ab7-fb441398f4d6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other security issues. In this case, the function `localtime` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to data races if used in a multi-threaded context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use thread-safe alternatives to unsafe C functions. In the case of `localtime`, a safer alternative would be `localtime_r` which is reentrant and thread-safe.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the unsafe `localtime` function with the safer `localtime_r` function. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\ntime_t rawtime = log_clock::to_time_t(msg.time);\nstruct tm * timeinfo;\nstruct tm timeinfo_buf;\ntimeinfo = localtime_r(&rawtime, &timeinfo_buf);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<ctime>`: for `time_t`, `localtime_r`, and `struct tm`\n- `log_clock` and `msg.time` are not standard C++ and seem to be part of a custom logging library. You would need to include the appropriate headers for these.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
