            {
              "id": "51590e07-6e15-3d90-a6db-ae9bfaf07c9a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential for causing buffer overflow. In C++, this often includes functions like `strcpy()`, `sprintf()`, `vsprintf()`, `gets()`, etc. These functions do not perform bounds checking, and can therefore overwrite memory, leading to unpredictable program behavior, crashes, or code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `vsnprintf()` is used, which is generally considered safer than `sprintf()` or `vsprintf()`, because it takes a size argument to prevent buffer overflows. However, it can still be misused or cause issues if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives whenever possible. For example, in C++, you can use `std::string` and `std::stringstream` for string manipulation, which automatically handle memory management and prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `vsnprintf()` function is used correctly, as it includes the size of the buffer as an argument. However, to further improve the safety of the code, you could consider using `std::vsnprintf()` instead, which is a safer version of `vsnprintf()`.\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdarg>\n#include <cstdio>\n\nvoid log(const char* format, ...) {\n    char logbuf[1024];\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    std::vsnprintf(logbuf, sizeof(logbuf), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    // log the message\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdarg>`: for handling variable argument lists\n- `<cstdio>`: for the `vsnprintf()` function\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "db6f45c6-f0ce-3105-bc47-be5e37fb6c31",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues, including denial of service, data corruption, and arbitrary code execution. This is why the use of `strcpy` is often prohibited in secure coding guidelines.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace `strcpy` with safer alternatives that include array bounds checking, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than a specified number of characters are copied to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(info->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name,\n        (name + std::to_string(i)).c_str(),\n        sizeof(info->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name) - 1);\ninfo->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name[sizeof(info->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncpy` copies at most `sizeof(info->peer_device[cnt].peer_device.name) - 1` characters from the source string to the destination buffer, and then manually null-terminates the destination string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `strncpy`\n- `<string>` for `std::to_string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9e6e2425-c7c5-30c7-9f37-6d2168d01707",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no checks ensuring that the source and destination are non-null, which increases the risk of null pointer dereference. There is also no explicit null-termination of the destination buffer after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings. The absence of any guard conditions or capacity checks further increases the risk. All these factors indicate a high likelihood of a real and exploitable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. Additionally, check that both pointers are non-null and explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying. Here is an example of how to address these issues:\n\n```cpp\nif (status && buf && status->vbios_part_number != buf) {\n    strncpy(status->vbios_part_number, buf, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1);\n    status->vbios_part_number[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis code checks that both the destination and source are not null, ensures they do not point to the same memory, copies up to one less than the maximum length, and explicitly null-terminates the destination buffer. This prevents undefined behavior and ensures the destination string is always null-terminated.\n\n## References\n- [C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6e46fbca-14d8-3eec-a8cb-a6ca02519e9c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null. The code copies a string representation of a version number into a destination buffer using a fixed maximum length, but there is no evidence of checks to ensure that the destination pointer is not null before the operation. The source is a string generated by the standard library and is unlikely to be null, but the destination pointer could be null, which would result in a crash or undefined behavior. The absence of a null check for the destination pointer is a significant risk factor. The probability of this being a false positive is very low, as the analysis did not find any guards or capacity checks that would mitigate the risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data into the destination buffer, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. This prevents undefined behavior or crashes if the pointer is invalid. For example, add a check before the copy operation:\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && fw_version->version) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->version, std::to_string(version).c_str(), AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis approach ensures that the destination is valid before performing the copy, and also guarantees that the string is null-terminated, which is a common best practice when using `strncpy`.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "31cca677-6581-308f-9ce7-78f57cafac97",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The destination and source are both variables (not string literals or constant data), and dataflow analysis suggests that the destination may alias the source. This can lead to unpredictable results or crashes, as strncpy is not safe for overlapping memory regions. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and the destination is a pointer type, which increases the risk. The source and destination are both confirmed to be non-null, so the main concern is the overlap, not null pointer dereference.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory where the source and destination may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(sku, token, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\n```\n\nIf you need to ensure null-termination, manually set the last byte to `\\0` after the copy:\n\n```c\nmemmove(sku, token, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN);\nsku[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "03101c76-87ae-392f-8b4e-03ed8979c416",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no checks ensuring that the source and destination are non-null, which further increases the risk of runtime errors such as segmentation faults. The absence of explicit null-termination after the copy operation also means that the destination buffer may not be properly null-terminated, potentially leading to string handling errors. These factors indicate a real and actionable security or correctness issue that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the string copy, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the source and destination pointers are not the same or do not refer to overlapping memory regions. Also, check that both pointers are non-null before the operation. After copying, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && buf &&\n    ((fw_version->version + AGA_MAX_STR_LEN <= buf) || (buf + AGA_MAX_STR_LEN <= fw_version->version))) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->version, buf, AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle error: null pointer or overlapping buffers\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
