            },
            {
              "id": "a23b1a9e-05e0-351d-9395-4c5c2757c690",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are related by data flow, which means they may refer to overlapping memory areas. This can cause unpredictable program behavior, data corruption, or crashes. Additionally, the analysis could not rule out the possibility that the source pointer is null, which would also result in undefined behavior. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent these issues, and the function verdict confirms this is a genuine issue. The destination is not a pointer-based member, reducing the risk of null destination, but the overlap and possible null source remain significant concerns.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior caused by overlapping memory regions, ensure that the source and destination do not overlap before performing the memory copy. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. For example, replace the original call with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(&key.id[8], &gpu_unique_id, sizeof(gpu_unique_id));\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you can guarantee that the source and destination do not overlap, document this assumption clearly in the code and add assertions or checks as appropriate. If the source pointer could be null, add a check before the copy:\n\n```c\nif (&gpu_unique_id != NULL) {\n    memcpy(&key.id[8], &gpu_unique_id, sizeof(gpu_unique_id));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "87d79ada-a631-372b-a642-6752f0bbde67",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Production Code Debugging Vulnerability\" in C++ is a type of software vulnerability that occurs when debugging code is inadvertently left in the production code. This can lead to a variety of security risks, including information disclosure, unauthorized access, and even remote code execution. In the provided code snippet, there doesn't seem to be a direct debugging vulnerability. However, if the port number (`AGA_DEFAULT_RDC_GRPC_SERVER_PORT`) is not properly validated or secured, it could potentially be exploited.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this type of vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n- Always remove debugging code or statements before deploying the application to production.\n- Use preprocessor directives or build flags to ensure debugging code is not included in the production build.\n- Regularly use static code analysis tools to detect potential vulnerabilities in the code.\n- Validate and sanitize all inputs, including port numbers, to prevent potential attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, ensure that the port number is validated and sanitized. For example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdexcept>\n\n#define MIN_PORT 0\n#define MAX_PORT 65535\n\nint validatePort(int port) {\n    if (port < MIN_PORT || port > MAX_PORT) {\n        throw std::invalid_argument(\"Invalid port number\");\n    }\n    return port;\n}\n\n// ...\n\nstd::string serverAddress = \"127.0.0.1:\" + std::to_string(validatePort(AGA_DEFAULT_RDC_GRPC_SERVER_PORT));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<stdexcept>` for handling exceptions.\n- `<string>` for string manipulation.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Review Guide](https://owasp.org/www-project-code-review-guide/)\n- [CWE-489: Active Debug Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/489.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS162092"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a771b9bd-d58a-3095-a5d8-bcad5fd9083c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities as it returns a pointer to a string that is stored in a static memory area. Any subsequent call to `getenv` will modify the same memory area, leading to potential race conditions if the application is multithreaded.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using unsafe C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library or other trusted libraries. In the case of `std::getenv`, consider using a thread-safe and reentrant function.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer alternative to `std::getenv` is to use the `std::getenv_s` function, which is thread-safe and reentrant. Here is how you can modify the code:\n\n```cpp\nchar* buffer;\nsize_t bufferSize;\n_dupenv_s(&buffer, &bufferSize, \"PERSISTENT_LOG_DIR\");\nstd::string path = (buffer ? std::string(buffer) : std::string(\"/obfl\")) + \"/\" + \"boot_count.txt\";\nfree(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>` for `std::getenv`\n- `<string>` for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c0caa3a3-ee54-39b1-a7df-4e918c06a257",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the function `localtime` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to data races if used in a multi-threaded context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in a multi-threaded context. Instead, use their thread-safe counterparts. In this case, use `localtime_r` instead of `localtime`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `localtime` function with its thread-safe counterpart `localtime_r`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\ntime_t rawtime;\nstruct tm * timeinfo;\n\ntime (&rawtime);\ntimeinfo = localtime_r(&rawtime, &timeinfo);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `ctime` for `time_t`, `time`, and `localtime_r`\n- `details::os` for `details::os::localtime`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5da22c50-53dd-3d81-b274-3f2ce9fc19c9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may overlap based on data flow, which can cause undefined behavior with functions like memcpy. Additionally, the source may be null at this call site, which further increases the risk. There are no explicit guards or checks to prevent these issues, and the verdict for this call site is marked as genuine. The destination is not likely to be null, but the overlap and possible null source are significant concerns.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(&key.id[0], &host_id, sizeof(host_id));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that even if the source and destination regions overlap, the operation will be performed safely without causing undefined behavior.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "777b308f-12d1-3226-8ac4-db9e906623c1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the destination buffer and the source expression may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the strncpy function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, the source may be null at this call site, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior. There is no explicit null-termination of the destination buffer after the copy, and no guard is present to ensure safe operation. The destination is not a pointer-based member, which slightly reduces the risk of null pointer dereference, but the overlap and null source risks remain significant. These factors indicate a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent potential security and stability problems.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the strncpy operation, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If there is any chance of overlap, use a memory-safe function such as memmove, which is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, always check that the source is not null before performing the copy, and explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after the copy to prevent unterminated strings.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (event_description_(args->events[e]) != nullptr) {\n    // Use memmove if overlap is possible\n    memmove(event_data[num_elem].message, event_description_(args->events[e]), MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE);\n    // Ensure null-termination\n    event_data[num_elem].message[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1240a99e-b466-3d1b-b255-eab9ebb39248",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee null-termination of the destination string when the source string length is greater than or equal to the size parameter. This can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, alter the intended control flow, read sensitive information, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination of the destination string. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `std::string` class or the `strlcpy` function if available. Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string and the null-terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with `strlcpy` or `std::string`. Here is an example of how to do it with `std::string`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string event_message(event_record.message);\nif (event_message.size() > AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR) {\n    event_message.resize(AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<string>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6386dba5-6783-3a4b-8be8-99a853a532ae",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is a possibility that either the source or destination pointer could be null, as there are no clear checks to prevent this. The function does explicitly assign a null terminator after the copy, which is good practice, but it does not mitigate the risk of undefined behavior from overlapping memory or null pointers. These factors indicate a real and actionable security or correctness issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying strings, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. If there is any chance of overlap, use a memory-safe function that supports overlapping regions, such as `memmove`, instead of the standard string copy function. Additionally, check that both the source and destination pointers are not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference.\n\nExample remediation:\n\n```cpp\nif (event.message != nullptr && event_buffer[i].message != nullptr) {\n    // Use memmove if overlap is possible\n    memmove(event.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n    event.message[AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "647724da-3b6f-3998-8fa6-9857a7e6afa4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The source and destination expressions are related by data flow, which means they may refer to overlapping memory. This is dangerous because strncpy does not guarantee correct behavior when source and destination overlap, potentially leading to data corruption or unpredictable results. Additionally, there is no evidence of checks to ensure that the source and destination are not null, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior. The explicit assignment of a null terminator after the call does not mitigate the overlap risk. The verdict is supported by the classification and the high-confidence 'GENUINE' label from the analysis.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the risk of undefined behavior when source and destination memory regions may overlap, ensure that the source and destination do not overlap before calling `strncpy`. If overlap is possible, use a memory-safe function such as `memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. For example:\n\n```cpp\n// Only use strncpy if you are certain src and dst do not overlap.\nif (!regions_overlap(event_record.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR)) {\n    strncpy(event_record.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n    event_record.message[AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    memmove(event_record.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\n    event_record.message[AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n\n// Helper function to check for overlap (implement as needed)\nbool regions_overlap(const void* dst, const void* src, size_t len) {\n    const char* d = (const char*)dst;\n    const char* s = (const char*)src;\n    return (d < s + len) && (s < d + len);\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you know that overlap is not possible by design, document this assumption clearly and add assertions to enforce it at runtime:\n\n```cpp\nassert(!regions_overlap(event_record.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR));\nstrncpy(event_record.message, event_buffer[i].message, AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR);\nevent_record.message[AGA_MAX_EVENT_STR - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7e25c873-e96c-332d-bccb-09d3b86b2035",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that is stored in environment block associated with the current process. If the environment variable is changed, the string can also be changed, leading to potential security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` function. Instead, consider using more secure alternatives that are less prone to vulnerabilities. Always validate and sanitize the input and output data. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `std::getenv`, consider using a more secure method to retrieve environment variables. For example, you can use the `std::getenv_s` function, which is more secure and less prone to vulnerabilities.\n\n```cpp\nsize_t requiredSize;\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, \"NON_PERSISTENT_LOG_DIR\");\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    printf(\"The environment variable doesn't exist.\\n\");\n}\nelse\n{\n    char* non_persistent_dir = (char*) malloc(requiredSize * sizeof(char));\n    if (non_persistent_dir != NULL)\n    {\n        getenv_s(&requiredSize, non_persistent_dir, requiredSize, \"NON_PERSISTENT_LOG_DIR\");\n        printf(\"NON_PERSISTENT_LOG_DIR = %s\\n\", non_persistent_dir);\n        free(non_persistent_dir);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This library is required for the `std::getenv` function.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5ebb6a9e-e79f-396b-9307-dc88eb918726",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that is stored in environment block associated with the current process. If the environment variable is changed, the pointer returned by `std::getenv` may become invalid.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` function. Instead, consider using more secure alternatives that are less prone to error. If you must use `std::getenv`, ensure that you validate and sanitize the input and output of the function. Also, ensure that the returned pointer is not directly used without checking its validity.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nchar* logstash_dir;\nconst char* temp = std::getenv(\"LOGSTASH_DIR\");\nif(temp != nullptr)\n{\n    logstash_dir = new char[strlen(temp) + 1];\n    strcpy(logstash_dir, temp);\n}\nelse\n{\n    // Handle the error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This library is required for `std::getenv` function.\n- `<cstring>`: This library is required for `strcpy` and `strlen` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b1ef4586-4b93-3e49-8025-a88efe4342b0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that is stored in environment block associated with the current process. If the environment variable is changed, the pointer returned by `std::getenv` may become invalid.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` function. Instead, consider using more secure alternatives that are less prone to error, such as `std::getenv_s` or platform-specific methods for retrieving environment variables. Always validate and sanitize the input from environment variables.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nchar* value;\nsize_t len;\nerrno_t err = _dupenv_s(&value, &len, \"PERSISTENT_LOG_DIR\");\nif (err) {\n    std::cout << \"Error occurred!\" << std::endl;\n} else {\n    persistent_dir = value;\n    free(value);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `std::getenv` function\n- `<iostream>`: for `std::cout`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
