            {
              "id": "e625a29e-e0ba-3b4f-a4e1-b6d216db1154",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null. The code copies a string representation of a version number into a destination buffer using a function that does not guarantee null-termination if the buffer size is reached. There is no evidence of a guard or check to ensure that the destination pointer is not null before the copy operation. The source is a string generated at runtime, but the main risk is the lack of validation for the destination pointer, which could lead to a crash or undefined behavior if it is null. The absence of a null-terminator assignment after the copy further increases the risk of string-related vulnerabilities. The analysis also notes that the destination pointer may be null, and there are no guards in place to prevent this. Therefore, this is a genuine issue that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Also, after using the copy function, explicitly set the last character of the buffer to a null terminator to guarantee proper string termination. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (fw_version && fw_version->version) {\n    strncpy(fw_version->version, std::to_string(version).c_str(), AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1);\n    fw_version->version[AGA_MAX_STR_LEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference and ensures the string is always null-terminated, reducing the risk of buffer overflows and string-related vulnerabilities.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
